n extremal value. A failed attempted argument is not by itself\\n125: evidence against the supplied proof.\\n126: \\n127: Unfolding an explicitly supplied definition and displaying its logical\\n128: consequence is a local derivation; it does not need a separate source theorem\\n129: merely because that consequence has a familiar name. Conversely, a familiar\\n130: name without the required definition, premises and derivation supplies no\\n131: fact. Check the actual argument and identify the specific unsupported step;\\n132: do not replace that check with a blanket objection to a named result.\\n133: Distinguish a genuinely partial construction from an explicitly total\\n134: operation: its stated type/convention controls which existence guards are\\n135: needed, and totality alone grants no intended analytic property.\\n136: \\n137: Authors construct arguments and their evidence together, then report their\\n138: own actual check outcomes. An explicitly unresolved or unperformed check\\n139: prevents release as a complete artifact. Finishing a checklist, retrieving a\\n140: source, passing a structural gate or asserting that conditions hold proves\\n141: none of the mathematical requirements. Audit A remains an independent actual\\n142: judgment and can find an omission the author did not recognize.\\n143: <!-- yui-nl0-acceptance:end -->\\n144: \\n145: ## Auditor execution protocol\\n146: \\n147: The sections below implement the shared requirements. Reviewer identity,\\n148: fresh packet replay, negative controls, evidence capture and verdict handling\\n149: are auditor/controller duties; they are not extra mathematical facts for an\\n150: NL0 author. Mathematical FAIL findings identify the applicable shared table\\n151: ID and the exact unsupported implication. If the permission boundary itself\\n152: is ambiguous, disclose that ambiguity rather than silently raising the bar.\\n153: \\n154: Use [self-containment](self-containment.md) and the\\n155: [audit template](../templates/audit-a.md). New acceptance requires spec/graph\\n156: v2 and audit v3. Historical PASS records do not satisfy these requirements.\\n157: \\n158: This audit does not use a successful project Lean build as evidence that the\\n159: NL proof is correct. Querying pinned mathlib for source signatures is allowed;\\n160: reusing the target's historical Lean proof/verdict requires explicit disclosure\\n161: and does not establish an independent NL audit.\\n162: \\n163: ## A0. Freeze and disclose\\n164: \\n165: Read the original problem and independently check `spec.json`: target,\\n166: quantifiers, all H roots, parameter ranges, definitions, representatives,\\n167: normalization, endpoint conventions, and one-target scope. No H may originate\\n168: from the proof writer's desired conclusion. Audit target semantics, not just\\n169: hash equality. Record reviewer/carrier, shared context, read artifacts, and\\n170: whether any reference solution was seen. Read the canonical graph, not only\\n171: its generated Markdown. Bind the receipt to spec/graph hashes.\\n172: Generate the exhaustive global index with\\n173: `python3 nl0/scripts/nl0.py overview nl0.json --spec spec.json --out global-overview.json`.\\n174: It preserves every actual H (including unused or late-appended H), full global\\n175: statements/definitions and dependency edges with canonical pointers and hashes.\\n176: Read this inventory before claiming a node/condition is absent; a partial line\\n177: page cannot establish absence. The index supplies no verdict and does not\\n178: replace inspection of the relevant canonical proof and primary-source content.\\n179: The clean test adapter uses this same core generator, not a separate inventory\\n180: or mathematical standard.\"}"}
{"i":2,"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/self-containment.md\", \"total_lines\": 147, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 147, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 74, \"requested_end\": 220, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # Self-containment details\\n2: \\n3: This is the detailed meaning of Audit A's `statement_closure`,\\n4: `object_closure` and `dependency_closure` requirements. The\\n5: [shared Audit A section](audit-a.md#shared-mathematical-acceptance-requirements)\\n6: is the acceptance authority for author and auditor; this annex supplies\\n7: interpretation and examples, not an independent permission policy.\\n8: \\n9: ## Two boundaries\\n10: \\n11: Accepted NL0 is a complete proof relative to its declared H/E/M roots, frozen\\n12: logical foundation and version-bound verified background notes. It is not an outline requiring the original manuscript to\\n13: recover missing definitions, formulas, assumptions, or arguments. E/M statements\\n14: are fully restated; their primary sources are inspected for provenance, not\\n15: used to supply unstated premises. The fixed library's own proof dependencies\\n16: need not be recopied. The target work's internal results must be proved here.\\n17: \\n18: Every R must also be checkable from its packet alone: the complete statements\\n19: of its direct inputs, their required definitions/declarations, its complete\\n20: output, its local declarations, and its own argument. Another R's proof body,\\n21: an ancestor not listed as an input, an ambient original H, an original-source\\n22: crosswalk, or remembered material is not an additional mathematical resource.\\n23: Whole-target/source-fidelity checks are separate from this local proof check.\\n24: Under verified-background-1, local review may additionally verify routine\\n25: pinned-mathlib facts using logged read-only queries and record them in the\\n26: receipt. They join this R's effective dependencies, without new H or a graph\\n27: rewrite. See [background notes](background-notes.md). Mathematical uncertainty\\n28: still blocks; ordinary correct elementary compression alone does not.\\n29: \\n30: Original theorem conditions are H nodes, including conditions that could be\\n31: encoded in a refined type. Parameters introduce ambient data/structures, not\\n32: an ambient stock of original hypotheses. For example, declare a vector and\\n33: put its required nonzero condition in H; do not write “nonzero vector” in its\\n34: parameter type and use that condition from an empty input list. The same rule\\n35: applies to characteristic/range conditions and equations. An R must list every\\n36: original H it actually uses. Standard structure operations/axioms and the fixed\\n37: finite-language rules remain available; no new substantive theorem is added.\\n38: \\n39: An output must be understandable and callable without reading its producer's\\n40: argument. Include the actual equalities/inequalities, binder types, hypotheses,\\n41: parameter windows, constant dependencies, representatives and a.e./pointwise\\n42: meaning. Global defined notation can be expanded from the packet. A formula\\n43: number, theorem name, or paragraph location cannot replace mathematical content.\\n44: Source locators belong in provenance fields. New mathematical prose uses direct\\n45: input IDs and previously proved local F IDs, not inherited numbered references.\\n46: \\n47: ## Object closure\\n48: \\n49: For every mathematical object actually appearing in a statement, definition,\\n50: local declaration, or proof, locate one legitimate declaration:\\n51: \\n52: 1. A frozen…3064 tokens truncated…al primitive selections may be\\n71:   `{symbol: ...}`; the controller validates selections and binds the complete\\n72:   core syntax with exact type/meaning (numerals, number systems and empty-set/top\\n73:   notation). This is not a subject vocabulary or a list of allowed theorems. A new\\n74:   Audit A requires the bound metadata. Author-defined permission descriptions\\n75:   and conflicting primitive meanings are rejected, not silently accepted;\\n76: - `pins`: `{lean_version, mathlib_commit, mathlib_root}`; the root is a local\\n77:   locator, the version/commit are scientific identifiers;\\n78: - `inputs`: evidence records `{path, sha256}` for the original problem/proof.\\n79: \\n80: The exact machine identifiers permitted in `logic.rules` and inference `rules`\\n81: are `direct`, `modus_ponens`, `implication_introduction`, `negation_introduction`,\\n82: `contradiction_classical`, `case_elimination`, `forall_introduction`,\\n83: `exists_elimination`, and `natural_induction`. Describe algebra/analysis in the\\n84: actual proof text; it is not a new trusted logical rule. These identifiers\\n85: describe proof packaging, not a substitute for establishing the implication.\\n86: \\n87: The `elementary-language-4` profile in `nl0/scripts/foundations.py` permits\\n88: displayed finite algebra/order calculations and ordinary logic. Its core syntax\\n89: contains no domain-specific vocabulary. Objects and notation from any subject\\n90: are parameters, D definitions or scoped locals, with explicit meaning, type and\\n91: dependencies; they cannot be hidden in primitives. Recognizable notation grants\\n92: no identity, existence or regularity. No global dictionary extension is needed\\n93: to represent a new subject.\\n94: For every substantive fact still register an E/M input or give its local proof.\\n95: \\n96: Older saved specifications retain their original bytes and symbol metadata for\\n97: read-only inspection under their recorded runtime. Starting a new Audit A under\\n98: this profile requires current canonical metadata: remove obsolete domain\\n99: primitive entries, declare any objects they actually supplied, and validate\\n100: the resulting source again. A new profile does not upgrade an old receipt.\\n101: \\n102: Definitions may be globally visible but guarded. Example: “K is the unique\\n103: fixed curve from N008” requires N008 before K is used. N008 itself must state\\n104: existence using the previously defined space/operator, without assuming K.\\n105: Proof prerequisites must be direct inference inputs in v2;\\n106: the output being proved cannot discharge its own definition prerequisite.\\n107: Definition prerequisites of input/output statements are checked too.\\n108: \\n109: ## Proposition fields\\n110: \\n111: Every proposition has `id`, `kind`, the complete `statement`, `definitions`\\n112: (D IDs), `binders`, and `objects`:\\n113: \\n114: - `binders`: `{symbol, type}` records for statement-bound variables. These do\\n115:   not introduce free variables in a consuming R. Register actual nested scope\\n116:   in the statement; alpha-rename to avoid global shadowing.\\n117: - `objects`: distinct names of mathematical objects actually used in the\\n118:   statement, from frozen parameters/primitives, referenced D closure or these\\n119:   binders. A D also has these fields for its defining expression.\\n120: \\n121: These are inventories, not a parser-generated list. Audit A must find omitted\\n122: objects by reading actual prose/formulas. Export all formulas and conditions;\\n123: another N ID or an original equation number cannot replace their content.\\n124: \\n125: | kind | ID | Producer | Additional fields |\\n126: |---|---|---|---|\\n127: | `hypothesis` | Hddd | none | exactly matches frozen H statement |\\n128: | `mathlib` | Mddd | none | `source` record below |\\n129: | `external` | Eddd | none | `source` record below |\\n130: | `internal` | Nddd | exactly one | `origin` locating source or explaining added bridge |\\n131: | `target` | P | exactly one | exact frozen `conclusion` |\\n132: \\n133: A source record contains `statement` (exact NL restatement), `evidence` (file\\n134: hashes), and `hypotheses` (complete applicability obligations, not their proof).\\n135: M additionally has `declaration`, `module`, `mathlib_commit`, `source_path`,\\n136: `source_sha256`, `signature` (complete Lean output), and `declaration_kind` equal\\n137: to `theorem` or `proved_instance`. A definition/opaque constant is not a proved\\n138: source proposition. Library definitions belong in D. A named M's official\\n139: library dependencies need not be copied into the project graph; pin the library\\n140: and inspect relevant axioms. Do not introduce project results as library M.\\n141: M evidence includes the successful version-1 report from `probe_mathlib.py`,\\n142: with the same ID, declaration, full signature, source bytes and toolchain pins.\\n143: In the clean Yui NL0 carrier, `probe_library` produces this evidence and the\\n144: submission adapter binds exact signature/source metadata from the successful\\n145: probe matching the selected M ID and actual declaration selector. Those metadata\\n146: fields may be omitted from that tool's submitted JSON; canonical graph files\\n147: still contain them in full. No unprobed source is filled from model memory.\\n148: The clean probe resolves genuine aliases to their canonical kernel name and\\n149: discovers the actual defining module. A requested module is only a locator\\n150: hint; its correction is recorded, never silently treated as source evidence.\\n151: Native type printing (and the legacy `#check @declaration` probe) preserves the complete outer telescope,\\n152: including implicit/typeclass conditions, using ordinary notation for nested\\n153: structure applications. Recursive expansion of inherited structure arguments\\n154: is unnecessary; omitted hypotheses or ellipsis are not permitted. The actual\\n155: kernel declaration kind, axioms, source bytes and version pins remain evidence.\\n156: For a local development revision the same tool also accepts\\n157: `{base_input: \\\"declared-task-ID.json\\\", patches: [...]}` when that explicit frozen\\n158: task document contains `{spec, graph}`. Patches are JSON data operations\\n159: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, or `test`, each with an absolute JSON pointer inside\\n160: `/spec/...` or `/graph/...` and a `value` except for `remove`. Array append uses\\n161: `/-`. An additional `replace_text` operation has `path`, `old` and `new`\\n162: instead of `value`: it replaces exactly one nonempty literal occurrence inside\\n163: an existing string. Missing or ambiguous matches are rejected; this is not a\\n164: regular expression or executable code. It preserves the rest of a long proof\\n165: paragraph without asking the model to retype it.\\n166: Every submission starts from the immutable named input, not the previous\\n167: failed candidate. The controller records the base hash and actual edits, then\\n168: performs the identical full source/structure checks. Successful output is still\\n169: the complete canonical `{spec,graph}`. This is an authoring convenience for\\n170: unaccepted candidates, not an incremental Audit A receipt or permission to\\n171: modify accepted/frozen source during a translation run. Actual mathematical\\n172: changes still invalidate affected reviews.\\n173: Within the same authoring stage, an actual check also returns its\\n174: `candidate_number`. Use `{base_candidate: N, patches: [...]}` to revise that\\n175: exact checked draft, including a structurally rejected draft. `read_draft`\\n176: can inspect a narrow JSON pointer in that own draft. These are the model's\\n177: own prior submissions plus bound metadata, not other tasks or local files.\\n178: Every edit still undergoes the full checker, and no draft inherits acceptance.\\n179: Resubmit an exact own draft with `{accept_candidate: N}` after a successful\\n180: check. Once a same-stage draft exists, returning to `base_input` requires an\\n181: explicit `reset_base: true`; this deliberately discards accumulated edits.\\n182: Without that explicit reset, use `base_candidate` so a later bookkeeping fix\\n183: cannot silently discard earlier mathematical corrections.\\n184: The adapter does not rewrite mathematical statements, hypotheses, definitions,\\n185: binders or arguments. Their fidelity remains the author's and auditor's work.\\n186: The following two explicit serialization forms copy the author's declared\\n187: data; they do not infer mathematics or grant Audit A acceptance:\\n188: \\n189: - An S `text`, D `definition`, or `spec.hypotheses[i].statement` may be `{from_task: \\\"declared-input-ID\\\", start_line: 10,\\n190:   end_line: 20}`. The controller copies exactly those inclusive lines of the\\n191:   already frozen same-stage input into an ordinary canonical string. It stores\\n192:   input/text hashes, the exact range and the canonical destination pointer.\\n193:   Only these proof/definition/hypothesis text fields have this form;\\n194:   undeclared files, other tasks and old candidates are not source IDs. Read the\\n195:   materialized draft, normalize source labels to legitimate input/F references,\\n196:   supply declarations/conditions and retain the complete argument. Copying the\\n197:   original proof does not establish closed-premise sufficiency. A copied\\n198:   definition still needs its complete declarations, dependencies and guards;\\n199:   it grants no mathematical property. A copied H must be a complete original\\n200:   GIVEN, with its origin retained; copying a desired conclusion or a claim\\n201:   from the proof does not make it a permissible hypothesis.\\n202: - `{base_candidate: N, source_uses: [{inference: \\\"R001\\\", at: \\\"S001\\\",\\n203:   input: \\\"M001\\\", bindings: \\\"actual substitution\\\", hypothesis_checks: [...]}]}`\\n204:   records existing M/E calls selected explicitly by the author. Each call adds\\n205:   its ID to that R's `inputs` and S's `uses`, and writes the supplied application\\n206:   record. Optional `id: Addd` distinguishes actual uses and is required for E.\\n207:   Exact replay is idempotent; a conflicting existing application must be\\n208:   explicitly patched. No new proposition/source or proof text is generated.\\n209:   Every normal check and actual Audit A still runs, including whether the\\n210:   theorem is actually used and each condition really follows from the packet.\\n211: \\n212: To avoid retranscribing mandatory duplicate strings, its tool input may omit\\n213: an H node's `statement` (copied from the same artifact's `spec.hypotheses`),\\n214: P's `statement` (copied from `spec.conclusion`), or an E/M `source.statement`\\n215: (copied from that proposition's primary `statement`). This is serialization of\\n216: one authoritative text, not a second premise or an inferred theorem. Canonical\\n217: files contain all fields. Conflicting supplied duplicates are rejected with\\n218: both texts shown; they are never silently overwritten. The actual external\\n219: `source_statement` and the probed Lean signature remain separate source-fidelity\\n220: evidence that Audit A must compare to the primary project restatement.\"}"}
{"i":4,"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/external-theorems.md\", \"total_lines\": 128, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 128, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 76, \"requested_end\": 160, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # External-theorem review: required Audit A standard\\n2: \\n3: Apply this standard to every retained E and every actual use of E in an R.\\n4: An E keeps the full source theorem; a derived specialization or substantive\\n5: translation is an internal N. Use the data contract's audit version 2. These\\n6: are reviewer obligations: a filled record or matching hash cannot establish\\n7: that a publication or a mathematical assertion is genuine.\\n8: \\n9: ## 1. Document identity\\n10: \\n11: Obtain and read the actual primary document. Match author, title, source kind,\\n12: publication/release information and exact edition/version to its provenance.\\n13: Record a traceable original source, retrieval date, local document and hash.\\n14: A search snippet, model answer, secondary citation, or functioning DOI/URL\\n15: alone does not establish identity. Books, papers and reliable preprints can\\n16: qualify; a DOI and journal publication are not universal requirements. Record\\n17: the source category and grounds for treating it as an admissible source.\\n18: \\n19: For missing originals, follow [ordered source retrieval](source-retrieval.md):\\n20: arXiv, Google full text, Google DOI discovery and confirmed-DOI Sci-Hub lookup.\\n21: Use normal browser readback when needed; unresolved access and verification\\n22: failures remain recorded. Then use the [conditional-source\\n23: procedure](conditional-sources.md) for unavailable text. Unavailable checks remain UNVERIFIED. A complete explicit\\n24: secondary statement can support a disclosed conditional run, not an ordinary\\n25: source-verification PASS.\\n26: \\n27: ## 2. Occurrence of the result\\n28: \\n29: Locate the claimed result in that document and version. Record theorem,\\n30: lemma, proposition or corollary label and page/section/paragraph/equation as\\n31: available; unnumbered results require an equally reproducible locator. Retain\\n32: the relevant primary text or a precise readback record bound to the document.\\n33: If the cited document merely points elsewhere without actually stating the\\n34: claimed result, follow and correct the source. An explicitly stated theorem in\\n35: an authoritative exposition can be cited as such; record its proof attribution\\n36: without claiming to have audited the referenced proof recursively.\\n37: Distinguish proved assertions from conjectures, questions, heuristics and\\n38: numerical observations. Preserve every condition of a conditional result.\\n39: \\n40: ## 3. Statement fidelity\\n41: \\n42: Compare the original full statement with the E statement, explicitly covering\\n43: objects, domains, scalar fields, spaces, hypotheses, regularity, quantifier\\n44: order, parameter intervals/endpoints, degeneracies, constant dependencies and\\n45: the exact conclusion (including a.e./everywhere, topology, uniformity,\\n46: existence/uniqueness and local/global scope). Record the notation map.\\n47: Faithful mathematical meaning, not word-for-word copying, is required.\\n48: Alpha-renaming is allowed. Never turn forall-x-exists-C_x into exists-C-forall-x.\\n49: \\n50: ## 4. Complete context and definitions\\n51: \\n52: Read the surrounding standing assumptions, definitions and conventions that\\n53: govern the result, including referenced context needed to understand it.\\n54: Capture any omitted chapter-wide hypotheses and normalization of measures,\\n55: norms, transforms, signs or scales. Map the source objects to the global D\\n56: ledger. Do not hide a condition outside the proof graph. A substantive\\n57: conversion requires an N proof; a renamed symbol is not that proof.\\n58: \\n59: ## 5. Version status and source eligibility\\n60: \\n61: Match every locator and statement to the chosen version. Check available\\n62: primary version histories, corrections, errata or withdrawal information at\\n63: the recorded date and scope. Record relevant findings and their effect;\\n64: explain use of an older version. Absence of a discovered correction is not\\n65: a guarantee that none exists. Unresolved conflicts remain UNVERIFIED.\\n66: Never import the target, an unproved project lemma, or a circular dependency\\n67: as E. Previously proved project modules require an expanded graph or a\\n68: versioned proof handoff. Do not attribute a combination of sources and a\\n69: project bridge to a single published theorem. A conjecture-dependent theorem\\n70: must retain that condition, which must be supplied legitimately at application.\\n71: \\n72: ## 6. Every actual application\\n73: \\n74: Give each actual E application an inference-local A ID. The same E applied\\n75: twice with different bindings has two A records, even within the same R.\\n76: For each A inspect the parameter/object bindings, prove every source\\n77: hypothesis from declared inputs or displayed local derivations, check types,\\n78: definitions and parameter ranges, identify substantive bridge N nodes, and\\n79: verify the resulting conclusion supports R's output. Record the evidence for\\n80: each individual hypothesis. “The theorem applies” is not a mapping.\\n81: Do not add an unproved hypothesis to close an application. An E passed at its\\n82: source does not automatically pass at any application site.\\n83: \\n84: ## 7. Ownership of derived work\\n85: \\n86: Put substantive specialization, scaling, change of normalization/domain/space/\\n87: norm/representation, combinations of sources, and prerequisite lemmas in N.\\n88: Each N lists its actual E/M/H/earlier-N inputs and its argument. Do not enlarge\\n89: the quoted theorem. Explicit elementary calculations and logical instantiation\\n90: may remain inside R; justify them rather than imposing needless graph nodes.\\n91: \\n92: ## 8. Separate decisions and reproducible evidence\\n93: \\n94: For each E write five separate review records: `literature_identity`,\\n95: `result_location`, `statement_fidelity`, `context_completeness`, and\\n96: `version_and_eligibility`. Each contains verdict, concrete notes and hashed\\n97: evidence. The first three must never be merged into one blanket PASS.\\n98: For each R/A write separate binding, per-hypothesis and conclusion reviews,\\n99: the source ID and the proved bridge IDs. Record actual reviewer, carrier,\\n100: shared context and reference material read. Self-audit is not independent.\\n101: \\n102: Use PASS only after the check was performed with adequate evidence; FAIL for\\n103: an established mismatch/omission/invalid application; UNVERIFIED when necessary\\n104: evidence is missing. Inaccessible text does not establish nonexistence. An\\n105: established absence of the claimed result in the cited source is a FAIL.\\n106: Retain failed records and the exact reason, source location and affected nodes.\\n107: \\n108: ## 9. Acceptance, invalidation and trust boundary\\n109: \\n110: Every retained E check and every application must PASS before the graph can\\n111: receive DRAFT-AUDIT-PASSED. A load-bearing FAIL blocks acceptance. Source-verification UNVERIFIED can\\n112: support only the explicitly qualified continuation above; other unresolved\\n113: mathematical checks still block it. Ordinary whole-proof acceptance requires\\n114: every retained check to pass. Never replace a\\n115: missing verification with a historical audit or a Lean build. Changes to the\\n116: source/version, statement, definitions, bindings or condition proofs invalidate\\n117: affected reviews; bind the replacement whole receipt to new canonical hashes.\\n118: \\n119: An inaccessible source suspends its citation verification, not the rest of\\n120: the workflow: continue available checks and explicitly conditional reasoning,\\n121: then report the unfinished citation checks and the UNVERIFIED overall audit\\n122: outcome. Descendants can be proved only relative to the explicit E assumption; the\\n123: whole result cannot receive an unconditional pass based on that source, and source inaccessibility alone is not a finding of mathematical error.\\n124: \\n125: List all external dependencies in the final verdict. This audit is relative\\n126: to those identified theorems; it does not independently reprove their published\\n127: proofs or establish a Lean proof of E. Mechanical checks establish record\\n128: coverage and byte consistency, not authenticity or mathematical correctness.\"}"}

